  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user's request: explain how relational DB executes SQL query from parsing planning through indexing joins transactions returning rows. Detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, common performance pitfalls. Complete sentences, avoid repeating. Need likely produce final answer only. Need be careful: final no hidden. Need write long technical tutorial. Need structure. Need include SQL query examples. Explain parsing, lexical, syntax, semantic analysis, optimization, physical plan, execution operators, indexing (B-tree, hash, covering, composite, partial, multi-column order, cardinality stats, MCV), joins (nested loop, hash, merge, index nested loop), transactions (MVCC, locking, isolation, WAL), returning rows (serialization). Tradeoffs and pitfalls. Need avoid repeating. Could use PostgreSQL-like terms but general. Need complete sentences. Let's plan.\n\nTitle: How a Relational Database Executes a SQL Query.\n\nIntro: A SQL engine turns declarative text into a set of physical operations. It must preserve semantics under concurrency.\n\nSections:\n1. Query text and preprocessing\n- lexer tokenizer, parser to AST, resolve names to catalog, type coercion, view expansion, CTE/with, security privileges.\nExample SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status='paid';. Need explain catalog. Errors.\n2. Logical plan\n- operators table scan, filter, project, aggregate, sort, join, distinct. Logical plan before access paths.\n- predicate pushdown, projection pushdown, commutativity/associativity? Optimizer rewrites.\n3. Statistics and cost model\n- histograms, most common values, distinct counts, row estimates, width. Cost components CPU, I/O, memory. Selectivity estimation. Correlated subqueries etc.\n4. Access paths / indexes\n- B-tree details: page tree, leaf sorted, keys, row pointers/tuples, range scans. Hash indexes: equality only. GIN/RT? maybe mention. Covering indexes (index-only scan). Composite indexes and leftmost prefix, ordering, partial indexes, expression indexes. Index maintenance overhead. Tradeoff: index speeds read, slows write, storage, optimizer may choose. Example composite (customer_id, order_date) for WHERE customer_id = ? AND order_date > ?; (status, created_at) if status selective and date range. Bad: index on low cardinality status alone. Index-only scan if needed columns in index.\n- Example: orders(order_id, customer_id, status, total, created_at",
